        "bodyTr": "A historic trilateral summit between the United States, China, and Russia will be held on the sidelines of the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation forum in Shenzhen, China on the 19th. The fact that the leaders of three nuclear-armed nations—U.S. President Donald Trump, Chinese President Xi Jinping, and Russian President Vladimir Putin—are gathering in one place is noteworthy enough to capture international attention. However, international concern outweighs optimism. There is deep anxiety that the nightmare of the 1945 Yalta Conference, where major powers restructured the world order while excluding stakeholders, could repeat itself. The three leaders approach the meeting with different calculations. President Trump, cornered by losses in the midterm elections, hopes to build diplomatic achievements through nuclear disarmament and a solution to the Iran conflict. President Putin would count it a success simply to escape his diplomatically isolated position due to the Ukraine war. President Xi seeks to seize diplomatic initiative but is reluctant to engage in disarmament discussions due to gaps in nuclear capabilities. With divergent interests, consensus will not come easily, but there is room for transactional diplomacy through give-and-take among great powers. The outcomes could have profound implications for Korean Peninsula security. The greatest concern is the possibility of South Korea being sidelined in discussions of the Korean Peninsula and international acceptance of North Korea's status as a nuclear power. While the White House has not excluded the North Korean issue from the agenda, North Korea is also making active moves. Kim Jong Un, who recently visited Dandong, has successively dispatched Kim Sung Nam, the party's International Secretary, to China and Choe Yong Won, his closest aide, to Russia. By aligning with China and Russia, North Korea can seek sanctions relief and the abandonment of denuclearization goals. After World War II, weak nations including Korea were treated as subjects of negotiation rather than agents of their own fate in discussions among great powers. This is why Ukraine views this summit as a \"second Yalta\" and Japan also worries about the possibility of closer U.S.-China-Russia ties. Foreign Minister Cho Hyun's statement that unilateral discussions on the Korean Peninsula between the United States and China are unacceptable is timely. The government must mobilize South Korea-U.S. coordination, trilateral cooperation among South Korea, the U.S., and Japan, and communication with China and Russia to uphold the principle of North Korean denuclearization. In particular, the government must secure American assurances that in North Korea-U.S. negotiations, denuclearization is not replaced by nuclear freeze or arms reduction. We hope that South Korea's diplomatic capabilities will be concentrated to ensure that the Shenzhen summit becomes a venue for building regional peace, not a repeat of \"Yalta,\" where the Korean Peninsula issue became a bargaining chip among great powers eighty-one years ago.",
