      "byline": "EDITORIAL",
      "body": "Jensen Huang’s departure exposes weak links in South Korea’s chip-driven economy A handshake can open a door; it cannot keep an industry on course. South Korea is confronting that distinction after Nvidia founder Jensen Huang’s abrupt departure, which has exposed how much of the company’s influence rested on one man’s personal authority. Nvidia announced on Thursday that Huang had resigned after 33 years as CEO. Colette Kress, executive vice president and chief financial officer, was named interim CEO. The decision followed reports of multiple extramarital relationships, including an alleged relationship with an executive at Singapore-based cloud computing firm Megaspeed. US authorities reportedly suspect the executive of links to Chinese intelligence. The allegations have turned a private scandal into a national security concern. For Korean businesses, the initial question is whether partnerships built around Huang will survive his departure. He cultivated ties with Samsung Electronics, SK hynix, Hyundai Motor, LG, Doosan and Naver, turning personal rapport into partnerships across the artificial intelligence supply chain. Korean conglomerates have announced cooperation in projects with Nvidia valued at roughly 660 trillion won ($494 billion). This figure represents the estimated scale of planned ventures, not guaranteed orders or committed spending. Nvidia’s new leadership will decide which commitments merit funding. Korean companies should ensure that relationships once advanced through Huang are secured by formal agreements and accountable teams. Nvidia’s influence over demand poses a broader concern. It has helped finance the cloud providers that buy its chips. A regulatory filing put its commitments to AI cloud partners at $36 billion as of July. Separately, Nvidia has worked with Wall Street investment firms on financing arrangements designed to channel up to $500 billion in third-party capital into AI infrastructure. Together, they show how chip sales are entwined with funding the data centers that consume them. That would matter in Seoul. Samsung and SK hynix together accounted for 83 percent of global high-bandwidth memory revenue in the second quarter. Their position means Korea has a central place in the AI supply chain, but also makes its fortunes sensitive to the spending decisions of a major customer. Korea’s semiconductor exports reached $173.4 billion in 2025, about a quarter of the record $709.7 billion in total exports. Washington adds another complication. Congress is considering the AI Overwatch Act, the MATCH Act and the Chip Security Act. The proposals would tighten oversight of advanced-chip exports, restrict allied sales of chipmaking equipment to China and require tracking mechanisms for chips shipped abroad. US House lawmakers have advanced the measures and are seeking to attach them to the fiscal 2027 defense authorization bill. The Megaspeed allegations have strengthened the case for tighter controls. For Korean manufacturers, the consequences could extend to equipment deliveries and servicing at factories in China. The disruption may also create an opening. Nvidia’s CUDA software ecosystem gives the company an advantage that a change of chief executive will not erase. Yet customers seeking alternatives may turn to AMD, Broadcom or custom accelerators from Google and Amazon. All require advanced memory, giving Korean suppliers an opportunity to expand their customer base by tailoring HBM packages to different chip designs rather than relying on Nvidia's specifications. Seoul and its conglomerates should preserve Nvidia partnerships while turning personal ties into binding commitments. They should find customers beyond Nvidia and coordinate with Washington over Korean plants in China. Public investment plans need to account for weaker AI demand rather than assume the current spending pace will last. Huang’s resignation does not spell the end of the AI boom. It does, however, expose the limits of an industrial strategy built around one executive’s judgment and personal network. Korea cannot predict the priorities of Nvidia’s next leader. It can decide how much of its industrial future to leave to a handshake. AI cheating case roils test preparation ahead of Suneung First confirmed case validates cheating suspicions, widening scrutiny of other national examinations [World Class] Sogang’s next global move pairs AI with a search for top scholars Kim Jong-un's top aide visits Russia ahead of US-China-Russia summit Ko Kyung-pyo vows to pause activities after drunk-driving crash From pills to orbit: Boryung heir's bold space bet Korean roots, foreign passports — living and working in Korea on an F-4 visa Survive & Thrive 5 things to know about Shin Min-a, K-drama's long-reigning sweetheart Stars Up Close The Ultrarich Lifestyle of the ultrarich Stars Up Close A spotlight on Korean artists who rose to prominence in unexpected ways, and moments behind their breakthroughs K-parenting 101 Dive into the world of Korean parenting Herald Interview A series of in-depth interviews. [Editorial] Rising demographic burden [Editorial] Flawed academy merger [Robert J. Fouser] The millennial wave arrives [Kim Seong-kon] Living in an absurd world is probably inevitable [Lee Moon-won] Wellness is a system; it needs maintaining Actor Ko Kyung-pyo caught drunk driving after truck crash AI cheating case raises scrutiny over test administration ahead of Suneung Tax accountant exam's top scorer admits to AI-assisted cheating Dressing rich, dreaming richer: Korea’s fascination with affluent lives Foreign student boom puts Seoul housing on global investors' radar",
